In this lesson, we'll look at expanding brackets when the number out the front is a fraction, or even negative, and you'll see it's really the same idea, you just have to slow down and be careful with signs. First, let's talk about fractional coefficients. Whenever you multiply a fraction by something in the brackets, you multiply just the top number and keep the bottom. So half of four x is two x, and half of negative six is negative three. Easy. Next, distributing a negative sign. Lots of people forget this, so listen up. The minus out the front means everything inside flips its sign. So negative three x minus five in brackets becomes negative three x plus five. Notice how the plus five flipped from minus five. Now, the trap. The biggest mistake is forgetting to multiply every single term inside, or forgetting that a negative flips the sign, or dropping a small unit like thinking half of twelve is five instead of six. Last one, put it all together. Negative two thirds times nine x plus twelve gives you negative six x minus eight. The trickiest bit is the twelve, because two thirds of twelve is eight, and the negative stays. So always ask yourself, did I touch every term? Did I flip signs where I needed to? Now let's see it in action.